            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Pre-Work: Confirm Account Permissions and Financial Links</h2>
<p>Before any fund operations, operations staff must complete the following checks:</p>
<ul>
<li>Confirm financial permissions on the ad account (top-up, view bills, download invoices)</li>
<li>Verify the billing cycle is pre-pay or post-pay</li>
<li>Check if the receiving account currency matches the top-up currency</li>
<li>Prepare common payment methods: corporate credit card, corporate bank transfer, third-party payment platforms</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Note</strong>: Some overseas platforms (e.g., Google Ads) have top-up limits for new accounts. It is recommended to keep single top-ups under $500 during the first week and increase limits after the account stabilizes.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 1: Standardize Top-Up Operations</h2>
<h3>1.1 Choose the Right Timing</h3>
<p>Do not wait until the account balance is nearly depleted to top up. It is recommended to set two-level alerts:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Alert Level</th>
<th>Trigger Condition</th>
<th>Recommended Action</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Level 1</td>
<td>Balance below 3x daily spend</td>
<td>Prepare for top-up, evaluate budget adjustment</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Level 2</td>
<td>Balance below 1.5x daily spend</td>
<td>Execute top-up immediately to prevent interruption</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>1.2 Execute Top-Up</h3>
<ol>
<li>Log into the ad platform backend, enter "Billing &amp; Payments"</li>
<li>Select "Add Funds" or "Prepay Account Top-up"</li>
<li>Verify account ID and company name to avoid wrong account top-ups</li>
<li>Enter top-up amount (recommended: 5-7x daily spend)</li>
<li>Choose payment method, confirm handling fees</li>
<li>Save top-up receipts, screenshot or download payment confirmation</li>
</ol>
<h3>1.3 Post-Top-Up Verification</h3>
<p>After funds arrive, operations must confirm:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Account balance updated, amount matches top-up record</li>
<li>[ ] Top-up timestamp recorded for subsequent reconciliation</li>
<li>[ ] If platform charges fees, record actual received amount</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Step 2: Balance Monitoring and Budget Allocation</h2>
<h3>2.1 Establish Balance Monitoring Sheet</h3>
<p>It is recommended that the operations team use a shared sheet (e.g., Google Sheets) to record:</p>
<ul>
<li>Account name and platform</li>
<li>Current balance</li>
<li>Last top-up time and amount</li>
<li>7-day average daily spend</li>
<li>Estimated depletion date (balance ÷ daily spend)</li>
</ul>
<h3>2.2 Multi-Account Balance Allocation Strategy</h3>
<p>When managing multiple ad accounts, prioritize funding for main accounts (highest ROAS or largest spend):</p>
<ol>
<li>Allocate funds based on spending ratio</li>
<li>Establish separate funding pools for new test accounts to avoid impacting main accounts</li>
<li>Conduct periodic (recommended weekly) reviews of account balances and spending trends</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Step 3: Spending Analysis and Budget Adjustment</h2>
<h3>3.1 Core Operational Metrics</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Metric</th>
<th>Calculation</th>
<th>Monitoring Frequency</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Daily Spend</td>
<td>Total expenditure for the day</td>
<td>Daily</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Weekly Spend Change</td>
<td>(This week - Last week) / Last week</td>
<td>Weekly</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Budget Utilization Rate</td>
<td>Actual spend / Budget limit</td>
<td>Daily</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CPM</td>
<td>Total spend / Impressions × 1000</td>
<td>Weekly</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CPA</td>
<td>Total spend / Conversions</td>
<td>Weekly</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>3.2 Abnormal Spending Identification</h3>
<p>Immediately investigate when the following occur:</p>
<ul>
<li>Daily spend increases by 30%+ but conversions do not increase proportionally</li>
<li>One ad group spends far more than others but has low CTR</li>
<li>Unusual spending activity during non-peak hours</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Step 4: Financial Reconciliation Process</h2>
<h3>4.1 Monthly Reconciliation Checklist</h3>
<p>At the end of each month, operations must complete with finance:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Download Statements</strong>: Export monthly statements from each platform (usually generated on the 1st-3rd of the following month)</li>
<li><strong>Verify Top-Up Records</strong>: Compare receipts against statement top-up details</li>
<li><strong>Confirm Spending Details</strong>: Summarize monthly total spend across accounts</li>
<li><strong>Calculate Balance Carried Forward</strong>: Opening balance + Top-ups - Spending = Closing balance</li>
<li><strong>Request Invoices</strong>: Most platforms support self-service invoicing; ensure invoice entity matches contract entity</li>
</ol>
<h3>4.2 Handling Reconciliation Discrepancies</h3>
<p>If statement amounts do not match top-up records:</p>
<ul>
<li>Check for pending top-ups (bank transfers typically have 1-3 business days delay)</li>
<li>Verify any refunds or credits that may not be included</li>
<li>Contact platform customer service with top-up receipts to request investigation</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Step 5: Risk Control and Compliance Management</h2>
<h3>5.1 Financial Safety Red Lines</h3>
<ul>
<li>Never share account permissions with unauthorized personnel</li>
<li>Large top-ups (single transactions over $5,000) require dual approval</li>
<li>Regularly update account passwords and payment passwords</li>
</ul>
<h3>5.2 Account Health Self-Check</h3>
<p>Conduct weekly checks:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Any expired payment methods on the account</li>
<li>[ ] Any unpaid outstanding amounts (common for post-pay accounts)</li>
<li>[ ] Any accounts restricted or frozen due to policy violations</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Summary</h2>
<p>The core of overseas advertising fund management lies in <strong>process standardization + real-time monitoring + standardized reconciliation</strong>. Operations teams only need to follow these five steps and maintain the habit of data recording in daily work to effectively avoid fund risks and reduce financial reconciliation errors. Teams should adjust inspection frequency and alert thresholds based on their account volume and spending scale.</p>
